Lab 19: Customizing User Roles in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Create and manage custom roles in OpenStack to reflect organizational policies.
Assign permissions to roles by modifying service policy files.
Map custom roles to users and groups for access control.
Implement least privilege access based on job functions.
Prerequisites
A working OpenStack environment (e.g., DevStack, OpenStack-Ansible).
Administrative access to the OpenStack CLI (openstack command).
Basic familiarity with Linux command line and YAML syntax.
Python installed for policy file editing.
Lab Setup
Access the OpenStack Controller Node
SSH into your OpenStack controller node:

ssh <username>@<controller-ip>
Verify OpenStack CLI Access
Ensure you can run OpenStack commands:

openstack token issue
Expected Outcome: A valid token with admin privileges is displayed.

Task 1: Define Custom Roles and Assign Permissions
Subtask 1.1: Create a Custom Role
Create a custom role (e.g., auditor):

openstack role create auditor
Expected Outcome: Role auditor is created with a unique ID.

List all roles to confirm:

openstack role list
Subtask 1.2: Modify Policy Files
Locate the policy file for the desired service (e.g., Nova):

sudo vi /etc/nova/policy.yaml
Add a custom rule for the auditor role (e.g., read-only access to instances):

"compute:get_all": "role:auditor or role:admin"
Key Concept: Policy rules use a "rule": "role:xxx" syntax.

Restart the Nova service to apply changes:

sudo systemctl restart nova-api
Task 2: Map Roles to Users and Groups
Subtask 2.1: Assign Role to a User
Assign the auditor role to a user (user1) in a project (project1):

openstack role add --project project1 --user user1 auditor
Verify the assignment:

openstack role assignment list --user user1 --project project1
Subtask 2.2: Assign Role to a Group
Create a group (audit_team) and add users:

openstack group create audit_team
openstack group add user audit_team user1
Assign the auditor role to the group:

openstack role add --project project1 --group audit_team auditor
Task 3: Implement Least Privilege Access
Subtask 3.1: Restrict Permissions
Edit the Keystone policy file to restrict admin privileges:

sudo vi /etc/keystone/policy.json
Modify rules to limit role creation to super-admins:

"identity:create_role": "role:admin and system_scope:all"
Subtask 3.2: Test Access Control
As user1, attempt to list instances (allowed for auditor):

openstack server list
Attempt to create an instance (should fail):

openstack server create --image cirros --flavor m1.tiny test-vm
Troubleshooting Tip: If access is denied, verify the policy file syntax and role assignments.

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Created custom roles (auditor) and assigned granular permissions via policy files.
Mapped roles to users/groups to enforce access control.
Implemented least privilege principles by restricting permissions based on job functions.
Next Steps
Extend this to other OpenStack services (e.g., Neutron, Cinder).
Explore role-based access control (RBAC) for multi-tenancy.
Cleanup (Optional)
Remove the auditor role:

openstack role delete auditor
